% 48d-self-hosting-hashtab.weft — Stage 8e: the FNV-1a hash table, written in C,
% compiled by kcc itself, and linked back into the compiler.

\chapter{Self-Hosting: The Hash Table in C}
\label{ch:self-hosting-hashtab}

The arena, the vector, and the string primitives ported \kcc{}'s foundation;
this chapter ports the associative structure built on top of it---the
open-addressing hash table of Chapter~\ref{ch:data-structures}, together with
its FNV-1a hash.  The table is how \kcc{} answers ``have I seen this name?'':
the keyword recogniser, the string interner, every lexical scope's symbol map,
and the per-function local and label tables are all
\texttt{kcc\_ht}s.  It is the first ported subsystem with real
\emph{algorithmic} content---a hash, a probe sequence, and a rehashing growth
step---so getting it byte-compatible exercises the compiler on loops, struct
arrays, and 64-bit unsigned arithmetic, all at once.

\section{The Byte-Exact Contract}

A hash table is only a drop-in replacement if it places every key in the same
slot the assembly did; otherwise a later pass that walks the table in slot
order would see a different sequence and the output would drift.  The port
therefore reproduces the algorithm exactly: the same FNV-1a constants, the same
\texttt{hash \& (cap-1)} starting index, the same linear probe with power-of-two
wrap, and the same growth trigger---double the capacity once inserting would
push the load factor past three-quarters, tested as
\texttt{(count+1)*4 > cap*3}.  The two 32-byte and 24-byte layouts match the
assembly field for field, so the header's \texttt{cap}/\texttt{count} and each
entry's \texttt{hash}/\texttt{key}/\texttt{keylen}/\texttt{value} sit where the
rest of the compiler expects them.  The port depends on the arena, \texttt{memset},
and \texttt{memcmp}, declared as \texttt{extern}.

@l c
@d Selfhost: hashtab preamble @{
struct HTEntry {
    unsigned long hash;
    const void *key;
    unsigned long keylen;
    unsigned long value;
};

struct HT {
    struct HTEntry *entries;
    unsigned long cap;
    unsigned long count;
};

void *kcc_arena_alloc(long size);
void *kcc_memset(void *s, int c, unsigned long n);
int   kcc_memcmp(const void *s1, const void *s2, unsigned long n);
@}

\section{FNV-1a}

The 64-bit FNV-1a hash folds each byte into a running accumulator: exclusive-or
the byte, then multiply by the prime, starting from the offset basis.  The
constants are the canonical ones, and the multiply wraps modulo $2^{64}$ as
unsigned arithmetic---exactly what the assembly's \texttt{mul} does.

@l c
@d Selfhost: hashtab fnv1a @{
unsigned long kcc_fnv1a(const void *data, unsigned long len) {
    const unsigned char *p = (const unsigned char *)data;
    unsigned long h = 0xCBF29CE484222325UL;
    unsigned long i = 0;
    while (i < len) {
        h = h ^ (unsigned long)p[i];
        h = h * 0x00000100000001B3UL;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return h;
}
@}

\section{Initialization}

\texttt{kcc\_ht\_init} arena-allocates the 24-byte header and a
\texttt{capacity}-slot entry array, zero-filling the latter so every key
pointer starts \texttt{NULL} (an empty slot).  The capacity must be a power of
two, so the mask \texttt{cap-1} selects the low bits of a hash.

@l c
@d Selfhost: hashtab init @{
void *kcc_ht_init(unsigned long capacity) {
    struct HT *h = (struct HT *)kcc_arena_alloc((long)sizeof(struct HT));
    unsigned long bytes = capacity * (unsigned long)sizeof(struct HTEntry);
    h->entries = (struct HTEntry *)kcc_arena_alloc((long)bytes);
    kcc_memset(h->entries, 0, bytes);
    h->cap = capacity;
    h->count = 0;
    return h;
}
@}

\section{Lookup}

\texttt{kcc\_ht\_get} hashes the key, starts at \texttt{hash \& mask}, and probes
forward.  An empty slot means the key is absent, so it returns~0 (a valid
``not found'' sentinel, since the table's stored values---token types, symbol
pointers---are never zero where a caller distinguishes them).  A slot matches
only when the cached hash, the length, and the bytes all agree, the cheap
checks gating the \texttt{memcmp}.

@l c
@d Selfhost: hashtab get @{
unsigned long kcc_ht_get(void *ht, const void *key, unsigned long keylen) {
    struct HT *h = (struct HT *)ht;
    unsigned long hash = kcc_fnv1a(key, keylen);
    unsigned long mask = h->cap - 1;
    unsigned long i = hash & mask;
    while (1) {
        struct HTEntry *e = &h->entries[i];
        if (e->key == 0) return 0;
        if (e->hash == hash && e->keylen == keylen &&
            kcc_memcmp(e->key, key, keylen) == 0)
            return e->value;
        i = (i + 1) & mask;
    }
}
@}

\section{Growth}

\texttt{kcc\_ht\_grow} doubles the capacity and rehashes.  Because the existing
keys are already unique, each is reinserted by probing the new array for its
first empty slot from \texttt{hash \& new\_mask}---no equality check is needed.
The entry is copied field by field, matching the assembly and sidestepping any
question of struct-assignment lowering.  Growth updates the header's pointer
and capacity in place, so every outstanding handle stays valid.

@l c
@d Selfhost: hashtab grow @{
void kcc_ht_grow(void *ht) {
    struct HT *h = (struct HT *)ht;
    struct HTEntry *old = h->entries;
    unsigned long oldcap = h->cap;
    unsigned long newcap = oldcap * 2;
    unsigned long bytes = newcap * (unsigned long)sizeof(struct HTEntry);
    struct HTEntry *ne = (struct HTEntry *)kcc_arena_alloc((long)bytes);
    kcc_memset(ne, 0, bytes);
    unsigned long mask = newcap - 1;
    unsigned long j = 0;
    while (j < oldcap) {
        if (old[j].key != 0) {
            unsigned long i = old[j].hash & mask;
            while (ne[i].key != 0) i = (i + 1) & mask;
            ne[i].hash = old[j].hash;
            ne[i].key = old[j].key;
            ne[i].keylen = old[j].keylen;
            ne[i].value = old[j].value;
        }
        j = j + 1;
    }
    h->entries = ne;
    h->cap = newcap;
}
@}

\section{Insertion}

\texttt{kcc\_ht\_put} grows first if this insertion would tip the load factor
past three-quarters, then probes: an empty slot receives the entry (and bumps
the count), while a slot whose key already matches has its value overwritten.
The grow-first order keeps the pointer and capacity it reads afterwards
current.

@l c
@d Selfhost: hashtab put @{
void kcc_ht_put(void *ht, const void *key, unsigned long keylen,
                unsigned long value) {
    struct HT *h = (struct HT *)ht;
    if ((h->count + 1) * 4 > h->cap * 3) kcc_ht_grow(h);
    unsigned long hash = kcc_fnv1a(key, keylen);
    unsigned long mask = h->cap - 1;
    unsigned long i = hash & mask;
    while (1) {
        struct HTEntry *e = &h->entries[i];
        if (e->key == 0) {
            e->hash = hash;
            e->key = key;
            e->keylen = keylen;
            e->value = value;
            h->count = h->count + 1;
            return;
        }
        if (e->hash == hash && e->keylen == keylen &&
            kcc_memcmp(e->key, key, keylen) == 0) {
            e->value = value;
            return;
        }
        i = (i + 1) & mask;
    }
}
@}

\section{Assembling the Translation Unit}

The fragments compose into \texttt{gen/selfhost/hashtab.c}, routed by
\texttt{assembly.weft}.  It supplies the whole \texttt{hashtab.s} object---the
hash and all four table routines---so the drop-in swap is complete.

@l c
@d Selfhost: hashtab @{
@<Selfhost: hashtab preamble@>
@<Selfhost: hashtab fnv1a@>
@<Selfhost: hashtab init@>
@<Selfhost: hashtab get@>
@<Selfhost: hashtab grow@>
@<Selfhost: hashtab put@>
@}

\section{Verification}
\label{sec:selfhost-hashtab-verify}

\texttt{make selfhost-hashtab} runs the drop-in loop: compile
\texttt{gen/selfhost/hashtab.c} with the assembly-built \kcc{}, relink a second
compiler with the hand-written \texttt{hashtab.o} replaced by the
kcc-compiled \texttt{hashtab\_c.o}, and re-run the code-generation suite.
Because every name the compiler resolves---keywords, identifiers, symbols,
labels---passes through this table, a green run means \kcc{} compiled its own
associative core correctly, hash and rehash and all.

@d Selfhost: hashtab verify @{
#!/usr/bin/env bash
BASE="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
# make selfhost-hashtab — compile kcc's hash table in C with kcc, relink,
# run the suite.
set -euo pipefail

SDK=$(xcrun --show-sdk-path)
SH=$BASE/selfhost
sources="main syscalls arena strings io hashtab vec strtab source lexer \
lexer_tables diag preprocessor parser expressions statements declarations \
types symtab conversions sema ir opt emit_arm64 emit_x86 linker codegen emit_macho dwarf driver"

echo "[1/3] compiling $BASE/selfhost/hashtab.c with kcc"
$BASE/kcc -c "$SH/hashtab.c" -o "$SH/hashtab_c.o" >/dev/null

echo "[2/3] linking $BASE/kcc_selfhost_hashtab (hashtab.o -> kcc-compiled hashtab_c.o)"
OBJ=""
for f in $sources; do
    if [ "$f" = "hashtab" ]; then OBJ="$OBJ $SH/hashtab_c.o"; else OBJ="$OBJ $BASE/src/${f}.o"; fi
done
ld -o $BASE/kcc_selfhost_hashtab $OBJ -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" -e _main -arch arm64

echo "[3/3] running the codegen suite with the self-hosted hash table"
cp $BASE/kcc "$SH/kcc_asm_backup_hashtab"
cp $BASE/kcc_selfhost_hashtab $BASE/kcc
set +e
bash $BASE/tests/test_codegen.sh
status=$?
cp "$SH/kcc_asm_backup_hashtab" $BASE/kcc
exit $status
@}
